{"availability":"available","full":"CodeRafay/Forensic-Image-Analysis-Toolkit","name":"Forensic-Image-Analysis-Toolkit","description":"Web app for image forgery detection. Runs 12 published forensic techniques: ELA, Splicebuster noise analysis, double-JPEG and JPEG ghosts, copy-move, PRNU camera fingerprint, resampling, histogram, LSB steganalysis, metadata/C2PA and more. Reports what each test measured and its limits, never a black-box real-or-fake verdict. Includes demo images.","category":"Developer tools","language":"Python","stars":14,"forks":5,"created":"2025-10-28T16:16:32Z","updated":"2026-10-05T16:56:48Z","license":"BSD-3-Clause","branch":"main","homepage":"https://veritas-forensics.streamlit.app","topics":["c2pa","computer-vision","copy-move-detection","digital-forensics","digital-forensics-tool-set","error-level-analysis","exif","forgery-detection","image-forensics","image-forgery-detection","jpeg","metadata-analysis","open-source","opencv","prnu","python","python3","splicing-detection","steganalysis","streamlit"],"demo":"https://veritas-forensics.streamlit.app/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.757Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.757Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.757Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.757Z","stars":14,"forks":5}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Veritas is a Streamlit web app that runs published image-forensics techniques on a JPEG or PNG and reports what each one measured: headline numbers, findings, maps and each test's stated limitations.","### ⚠️ Indicators, not proof Every result is an indicator, not proof. Each test detects one kind of trace under stated conditions; a clean result means only that this test found no inconsistency at its sensitivity. Veritas produces no authenticity score and no overall verdict: no combination of these tests can certify that an image is unedited. Its output is not evidence of authenticity and the hash ledger is not a legal chain of custody. Combine several independent tests with the image's context before drawing conclusions.","### Classical methods only Veritas deliberately uses classical (non-learned) methods so it runs on CPU within Streamlit Community Cloud's memory limits and ships under a permissive licence. Trained networks such as TruFor / Noiseprint++ (Guillaro et al., CVPR 2023), CAT-Net (Kwon et al., IJCV 2022) and CLIP-based synthetic-image detectors (Cozzolino et al., CVPR-W 2024) currently outperform these methods, especially after recompression and for AI-generated images. They need GPUs or several GB of RAM and their weights are mostly licensed for research use only. For high-stakes casework, use…"],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/CodeRafay/Forensic-Image-Analysis-Toolkit/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"2b1d366ca611acfca1c5091c814c0fe9bc97979279468846816e870333d53c84","extractedAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.896Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.896Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Veritas is a Streamlit web app that runs published image-forensics techniques on a JPEG or PNG and reports what each one measured: headline numbers, findings, maps and each test's stated limitations.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/CodeRafay/Forensic-Image-Analysis-Toolkit/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"2b1d366ca611acfca1c5091c814c0fe9bc97979279468846816e870333d53c84","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/CodeRafay/Forensic-Image-Analysis-Toolkit/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.896Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-10-05T16:56:48Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/CodeRafay/Forensic-Image-Analysis-Toolkit/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-12T08:00:30.896Z","hash":"2b1d366ca611acfca1c5091c814c0fe9bc97979279468846816e870333d53c84","originalBytes":12697,"storedBytes":12697,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["test","des